o  the ideological and hetero and  diagnostic heterogeneity and depression  there are a lot of studies now that  suggest that depression can be related  to alterations in a variety of  biological systems shown here on the  slide  and so it's really unlikely to be a  single disorder and this  as many know led the nih to develop a  research framework for investigating  mental disorders that integrate across  these many levels of information to  explore basic dimensions of functioning  rather than the disorders themselves so  for example anhedonia or dysphoria or  cognitive dysfunction or problems with  arousal or regulatory systems that cross  over diagnostic boundaries  novel  technologies such as cortical or  subcortical brain stimulation are  focused on circuit dysfunction models of  depression and so they're a potential  solution for patients that are resistant  to all available therapies particularly  because symptom dysfunction can be  mapped to dysfunctional circuits and  then specifically targeted  so intracranial brain stimulation such  as deep brain stimulation has shown  promise as a treatment for refractory  depression  in this treatment a pulse generator is  surgically placed in the chest or in the  skull and it delivers stimulation  through implanted electrodes to an area  of the brain so it typically targets a  single structure in the brain in all  subjects with largely a fixed and  constant stimulation dosing and this  approach has been successful in  parkinson's disease and epilepsy  dbs emerged in 2003  led by the pioneering work of helen  neighbor and in open label trials  patients even with the most severe  depression achieved remission and so  there was a really a great deal of  excitement because it was one of the  first treatments that targeted specific  brain circuits  but unfortunately these results were not  consistently replicated across three  randomized controlled studies and so the  figure here that i'm showing  shows a trial of ventral capsule  stimulation where advocacy was assessed  at the 16 week time point  and the lack of difference here between  the active and the control conditions it  may be due to the trial design which is  some people suggest that the 16 time  point 16 week time point may be in  retrospect too early however  inter-individual  response heterogeneity also likely  played a role and so novel strategies in  intracranial brain  treatment stimulation are needed rather  than abandoning the treatment altogether  so personalization of therapy has been  proposed as a means to  um out to improve the outcome of  medicine in general and it's been shown  to be able to do that the variable  presentation of symptoms across  individuals with depression is evidence  that different neural circuits may  underlie different depression biotypes  and it speaks to the need for  personalization within  depression as well  and in this image  from leanne williams work she highlights  specific circuits underlying specific  depression symptoms and suggests that  knowing the neural circuits involved for  each patient might inform our clinical  decisions for that patient and we argue  that this type of personalization is  also needed in brain stimulation as well  and in fact multiple anatomical  stimulation targets have been identified  and shown to have promise in small  studies and these are shown here on the  left and include  areas such as the sub-genial cingulate  and the ventral capsule which are common  structures but now other regions as well  regions within the reward circuitry  the ofc for example  as well and the fact that stimulation  across these multiple sites can improve  symptoms and different sets of people  suggest that personalization in brain  stimulation is possible  so i'll talk briefly about the two most  common dbs targets the majority of work  has been done in the sub general  cingulate by helen mayberg  building on the cortical limbic  dysregulation hypothesis of depression  and