ay to read out the  effect that the stimulation has on the  circuit you're targeting  but it's also possible to implant  microelectrodes arrays or leads that can  continuously record activity or sense  and that's what's being shown here  is a patient with spinal cord injury who  has an array planted in m1 that senses  neural activity and when one particular  pattern is observed it can move this  experimental arm  and you know later on a patient's arms  own arm itself  so you can see from this a  personalization of therapy includes not  only where you stimulate but also when  you stimulate and with motor disorders  you have visual feedback you can see  that the arm is moving or the tremor  parkinson's disease is reduced but in  depression you can't necessarily  visualize an emotion or a thought you  have to rely on subjects or physicians  reports of symptom severity state at  each moment in time but we can apply the  same principles  so with the addition of neural sensing a  patient's own physiological activity can  selectively trigger stimulation when a  pathologic state is detected and then  you can read out the effect that the  stimulation has on the circuit that  you're targeting and so that's the idea  behind closed loop deep brain  stimulation stimulation is  delivered only when symptoms severity is  high which  may reduce side effects prevent  adaptation and ultimately improve  efficacy of this type of treatment  there are a couple of devices that have  the capability to continuously sense  neural activity so what i'm showing here  is the fda approved and commercially  available neural pace rns system which  is the one we were using in our study  but the biggest challenge for closed  loop deep brain stimulation and  psychiatric disorders is finding a  symptom-specific neural biomarker that  can trigger stimulation and a biomarker  like this has not previously been found  for depression  but there are ways to do this so direct  neural recordings and focal elect