ility  our study population focuses on patients  with treatment resistant depression  the major inclusion criteria to know  that are different than previous trials  are  one we require evidence that neural  circuitry can be modulated as evidenced  by a response to ect but not a  sustained response so patients may  respond after one or two treatments but  they need to  keep getting ect to see have the benefit  and then two that we require that  there's sufficient variability in  self-reported symptoms that um and this  is a requirement because we need to  identify the biomarker and this helps us  do that  so here's the overall study trial design  as i mentioned the first stage of the  trial is the implantation of the 10  electrodes across 10 different brain  locations within the cortical limbic  circuitry the patient stays 10 days  in the hospital or laboratory setting  and we conduct stimulus response mapping  to identify the brain target where  stimulation best improves depression  symptom severity and then we also pair  resting state neural activity with  clinical depression scales to identify  spectral biomarkers  of a high symptom burden  so if we were able to find a brain site  where stimulation leads to a consistent  change in clinical symptoms then  participants meet criteria to move on to  the second stage  so here i am showing the results of the  10-day clinical mapping study for our  first patient so during this time we  performed bipolar stimulation survey  across the electrodes in the ventral  capsule the sub-genial cingulate  hippocampus amygdala and the orbital  frontal cortex  and what we saw is is really a diversity  of very specific responses across  different stimulation sites and  stimulation parameters and we just  delivered 90 seconds of stimulation and  we could see  a really  broad array of responses so some  examples of this included tingles of  pleasure with a hundred hertz central  capsule stimulation neutral alertness i  feel less cotton in cobwebs she said  with a hundred hertz stimulation and  subgenual cingulate she experienced a  calm pleasure she described as like  reading a good book with one hertz low  frequency stimulation in the ofc  and it was really astonishing to see  these effects and and through this  experience over the course of the 10  days we really learned the road roadmap  to her brain to the point where we could  predict the resulting emotion or mood  that  would develop prior to the stimulation  itself and i think it was an incredible  experience for the patient as well who  had really been stuck in one state for  years and years and to suddenly have  stimulation that changes your mood and  to suddenly feel relief was just  extremely life-changing for her  so after we  did the stimulus response mapping which  is just brief and and the patient knows  what we're doing we wanted to further  explore the brain sites and the  stimulation parameters that elicit the  positive response was sham controlled  stimulation so here we assessed the  effective stimulation using a couple of  different clinical measures we used a  five-point likert scale combining  subjective responses with  physician-rated affect we used visual  analog scales of depression anxiety  energy and we used the six uh question  subscale of the handy 17 to assess  the short term changes in depressions  vary so results from these scales  across our different trials suggested  that stimulation in this patient largely  modified two dimensions the arousal  dimension and the mood valence to men  dimension  and we found  that there were three different  stimulation paradigms in the patient  that all reliably improved mood but were  associated with different effects on the  level of arousal or activation  and that's what's shown here  you can see um in this  rectangle here you can see arousal in in  dark blue  and  move valence in blue here  and then somatic symptoms in pink or  peachy orange  and then in the second  row of the